{"name":"Stadium Grass","hex":"#d5f534","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"stadium","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A venue where sporting events are held.","An Ancient Greek racecourse, especially, the Olympic course for foot races."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stadium"},{"term":"grass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the family Poaceae, characterized by leaves that arise from nodes in the stem and leaf bases that wrap around the stem, especially those grown as ground cover rather than for grain.","Any of the various plants that are not in the family Poaceae that resemble grasses."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To lay out on the grass; to knock down (an opponent etc.).","To act as a grass or informer, to betray; to report on (criminals etc) to the authorities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Stadium","blocks":[{"text":"A stadium is a place or venue for (mostly) outdoor sports, concerts, or other events and consists of a field or stage completely or partially surrounded by a tiered structure designed to allow spectators to stand or sit and view the event. Pausanias noted that for about half a century the only event at the ancient Greek Olympic festival was the race that comprised one length of the stadion at Olympia, where the word \"stadium\" originated. Most of the stadiums with a capacity of at least 10,000 are used for association football. Other popular stadium sports include gridiron football, baseball, cricket, the various codes of rugby, field lacrosse, bandy, and bullfighting. Many large sports venues are also used for concerts. Many modern designs are increasingly incorporating sustainability features such as energy efficiency and proper waste management systems."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Narendra_Modi_Stadium_view_from_the_gallery.jpg/330px-Narendra_Modi_Stadium_view_from_the_gallery.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stadium"},{"title":"Grass","blocks":[{"text":"Grass refers to various families of plants. The three major families of grasslike plants are true grasses (Poaceae), sedges (Cyperaceae), and rushes (Juncaceae). Lawns and pasturelands are typically composed of true grasses, five of which cover 46% of the world's arable land: rice, wheat, maize, barley, and sugar cane."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Poa_annua.jpg/330px-Poa_annua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grass"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Ancient_Olympia_Stadium_in_Greece_%2851224128585%29.jpg/500px-Ancient_Olympia_Stadium_in_Greece_%2851224128585%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Ancient_Olympia_Stadium_in_Greece_%2851224128585%29.jpg/1280px-Ancient_Olympia_Stadium_in_Greece_%2851224128585%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Ancient_Olympia_Stadium_in_Greece_%2851224128585%29.jpg/500px-Ancient_Olympia_Stadium_in_Greece_%2851224128585%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Stadium at Olympia were built ~776 BC","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAncient_Olympia_Stadium_in_Greece_(51224128585).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Circus_Maximus%2C_plan_de_Rome_de_Paul_Bigot%2C_universit%C3%A9_de_Caen_MRSH.JPG/500px-Circus_Maximus%2C_plan_de_Rome_de_Paul_Bigot%2C_universit%C3%A9_de_Caen_MRSH.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Circus_Maximus